QFT-in-Curved-Space-Time/Checklist/OPE-f.content:
We start by expanding X_{13} in the limit of X_{12}&lt;&lt;X_{23},X_{13}:
\abs{X_{13}}^2 = \abs{X_{12}}^2+\abs{X_{23}}^2 +2X_{12}\cdot X_{23}
Now we define:
\epsilon \coloneqq \frac{\abs{X_{12}}}{\abs{X_{23}}}\qquad \cos \alpha \coloneqq \frac{X_{12}\cdot X_{23}}{\abs{X_{12}}\abs{X_{23}}}
\epsilon \cos\alpha = \frac{X_{12}\cdot X_{23}}{\abs{X_{23}}^2}
so that:
\abs{X_{13}}^2 = \abs{X_{23}}^2\left(1+2\epsilon\cos\alpha+\epsilon^2\right) 
Now we expand the term inside the parenthesis when elevated to a negative power up to second order:
\left(1+2\epsilon\cos\alpha+\epsilon^2\right)^{-k} \overset{\epsilon\ll 1}{\simeq} 1-2\epsilon k\cos\alpha+\epsilon^2 k(k+(1+k)\cos(2\alpha))
In our case, k=h_{312}. Recall the 3PF for scalars:
\langle \phi _1(x_1)\phi _2(x_2)\phi _3(x_3)\rangle =\frac{\lambda_{\phi_1\phi_2\phi_3}}{X_{12}^{2h_{123}}X_{23}^{2h_{231}}X_{13}^{2h_{132}}}
We apply the expansion to the right-hand side:
\frac{\lambda_{\phi_1\phi_2\phi_3}}{X_{12}^{2h_{123}}X_{23}^{2h_{231}}X_{13}^{2h_{132}}}\overset{\epsilon\ll 1}{\simeq}\frac{\lambda_{\phi_1\phi_2\phi_3}}{X_{23}^{2\Delta_3}}\frac{1}{X_{12}^{2h_{123}}}\left(1-2\epsilon h_{312}\cos\alpha-\epsilon^2 h_{312}\left(1-(1+h_{312})\cos^2\alpha\right)\right)
Now, in order to find the OPE expansion, we need to perform power matching between the last expression and:
\langle \phi _1(x_1)\phi _2(x_2)\phi _3(x_3)\rangle = C ^{\phi _3}_{\phi _1,\phi _2}(X_{12},\partial _2)\frac{1}{X_{23}^{2\Delta _3}}
The first term is easy:
C^{(0)} = \frac{\lambda_{\phi_1\phi_2\phi_3}}{X_{12}^{2h_{123}}}
The second term requires calculating a first derivative:
\partial _2 ^\mu \frac{1}{X_{23}^{2\Delta_3}} = \frac{1}{X_{23}^{2\Delta_3}}\left(-\frac{2\Delta_3 X_{23}^\mu}{X_{23}^2}\right)
Then we obtain:
C^{(1)} = \frac{\lambda_{\phi_1\phi_2\phi_3}h_{312}}{X_{12}^{2h_{123}}} \frac{X_{12}\cdot\partial_2}{\Delta_3}
The third term requires calculating second derivatives:
\partial _2 ^\nu\partial _2 ^\mu \frac{1}{X_{23}^{2\Delta_3}} = -\frac{2\Delta_3}{X_{23}^{2\Delta_3}}\frac{1}{X_{23}^2}\left(\eta^{\mu\nu}+\left(1-\Delta_3\right)\frac{2X_{23}^\mu X_{23}^\nu}{X_{23}^2}\right)
We perform power matching by considering the combination:
AX_{12}^\mu X_{12}^\nu\partial _2 ^\nu\partial _2 ^\mu \frac{1}{X_{23}^{2\Delta_3}}+BX_{12}^2\partial _{2,\mu}\partial _2 ^\mu \frac{1}{X_{23}^{2\Delta_3}}
and solving the following system for A,B:
\left\{
\begin{array}{l}
A\left(-4\Delta_3 (1-\Delta_3)\right) = 2h_{312}(1+h_{312})\\
-2A\Delta_3-2B\Delta_3(d+2(1-\Delta_3)) = -h_{312}
\end{array}
\right.
which yields:
A = \frac{h_{312}(1+h_{312})}{2(\Delta_3-1)\Delta_3}
B = \frac{h_{312} (2+h_{312}-\Delta_3)}{2(2+d-2\Delta_3)(\Delta_3-1)\Delta_3}
Then we have:
C^{(2)} = \frac{\lambda_{\phi_1\phi_2\phi_3}}{X_{12}^{2h_{123}}} \left(AX_{12}^\mu X_{12}^\nu\partial_{2,\nu}\partial_{2,\mu}+BX_{12}^2\partial_{2,\mu}\partial_{2}^\mu\right)

QFT-in-Curved-Space-Time/Conformal-block.content:
Consider 4PF of real scalar field \phi with \Delta = \Delta _\phi, we know that they are not completely fixed:
\corr{\phi (x_1)\phi (x_2)\phi (x_3)\phi (x_4)} = \frac{1}{X_{12}^{\Delta_\phi}X_{34}^{\Delta_\phi}}g(u,v)
where:
u\coloneqq \frac{X_{12} ^2X_{34} ^2}{X_{13} ^2X_{24} ^2}
v\coloneqq \frac{X_{14} ^2X_{23} ^2}{X_{13} ^2X_{24} ^2}
We can use OPE’s to reduce this expression. In principle this will be a sum over all primary operators, but we can prune some terms by recalling that an operator appears in OPE if and only if the related 3PF is non 0 and that only TST’s can have a non 0 3PF with 2 scalars (see OPE properties):
\corr{\phi\phi O^{\mu_1...\mu_l}}\neq 0 \Leftrightarrow O \textrm{ is TST and } l\textrm{ is even}
Knowing this,
\phi (x_1)\times\phi (x_2) \sim \sum _{\Delta, l\textrm{ even}} \frac{\lambda_{\Delta,l}}{X_{12}^{2\Delta _\phi -\Delta _O}}C_{\mu_1,...\mu _l} (X_{12}, \partial _2))O_{\Delta ,l} ^{\mu_1,...\mu _l} (x_2)
We apply the OPE twice:
\corr{\phi (x_1)\phi (x_2)\phi (x_3)\phi (x_4)}= \sum _{\substack{\Delta, l\textrm{ even}}} \frac{\lambda_{\Delta,l}}{X_{12}^{2\Delta _\phi -\Delta }}\frac{\lambda_{\Delta&#039;,l&#039;}}{X_{34}^{2\Delta _\phi -\Delta &#039;}}C_{\mu_1,...\mu _l} (X_{12}, \partial _2))C_{\nu_1,...\nu _{l&#039;}} (X_{34}, \partial _4))\corr{O_{\Delta ,l} ^{\mu_1,...\mu _l} (x_2)O_{\Delta &#039; ,l&#039;} ^{\nu_1,...\nu _{l&#039;}} (x_4)}
since 2PF’s are diagonal
\corr{O_{\Delta ,l} ^{\mu_1,...\mu _l} (x_2)O_{\Delta &#039; ,l&#039;} ^{\nu_1,...\nu _{l&#039;}} (x_4)} = \delta _{\Delta\Delta&#039;}\delta _{ll&#039;}\frac{T^{\mu_1,...\mu _l,\nu_1,...\nu _{l}}(x_2,x_4)}{X_{24}^{2\Delta}}
we get:
g_{\Delta,l}(u,v) \equiv X_{12} ^{\Delta}X_{34}^\Delta C_{\mu_1,...\mu _l} (X_{12}, \partial _2))C_{\nu_1,...\nu _{l}}(X_{34}, \partial _4))\frac{T^{\mu_1,...\mu _l,\nu_1,...\nu _{l}}(x_2,x_4)}{X_{24}^{2\Delta}}
Note that g needs to be a function of invariants
\corr{\phi (x_1)\phi (x_2)\phi (x_3)\phi (x_4)} = \frac{1}{X_{12}^{2\Delta _\phi}}\frac{1}{X_{34}^{2\Delta _\phi}}\sum _{\Delta,l} \lambda ^2_{\Delta ,l} g _{\Delta ,l}(u,v) 
However ugly the form of g may be, it’s built up from blocks we already know: ==its dependence on coordinates is totally fixed by conformal symmetry. The information about the theory is contained in the \lambda‘s and the sum over irreps.==
We call g a conformal block. This formalism leads to graph representation (contributions to a 4PF are given by sum over irreps):

QFT-in-Curved-Space-Time/Differential-ge.content:
Similarly to tangent fields, cotangent fields \omega map a manifold into the corresponding cotangent manifolds:
\omega: \mathcal{M}\mapsto\mathcal{T}^*\mathcal{M}
We can define the set of all cotangent fields:
{\Omega}^1[\mathcal{M}] \coloneqq\left\{\omega: \mathcal{M}\mapsto\mathcal{T}^*\mathcal{M}\right\}

We can define differentiable curves on a manifold \mathcal{M} with a chart (u,\varphi) as
C:(a,b)\subseteq \mathbb{R}\mapsto \mathcal{M}
\varphi\circ C \in C^1

We can define a differentiable curve C(t) on a manifold \mathcal{M} starting from its derivative at each time t, described by an element of the tangent space \mathcal{T}_{C(t)}\mathcal{M} at that point.
Taking a valid chart (u_t,\varphi_t) at each time, we can get the coordinates:
x(t)\coloneqq \varphi_t(C(t))
The local change of the coordinates is encoded by a local vector, or even better, a vector field, considering that we have to take into account the whole manifold:
\left\{
\begin{array}{l}
\frac{d }{dt}x^\mu (t) = V^\mu (x(t))\\
x^\mu (0) =x_0^\mu
\end{array}
\right.
We then define a flow \sigma generated by V^\mu:
\sigma ^\mu :\mathbb{R}\times\mathcal{M}\mapsto\mathcal{M}
which allows us to shift the dependence on x_0^\mu as a parameter:
x(t) \coloneqq \sigma ^\mu (t,x_0)
and by definition:
\left\{
\begin{array}{l}
\frac{d }{dt}\sigma^\mu (t,x_0) = V^\mu (\sigma(t,x_0))\\
\sigma^\mu (0,x_0) =x_0^\mu
\end{array}
\right.
Composition
Noting that:
\frac{d }{dt}\sigma^\mu (t+s,x_0) = \frac{d \sigma^\mu (t+s,x_0)}{d(t+s)}\frac{d (t+s)}{dt} = V^\mu (\sigma(t+s,x_0))
\sigma^\mu (0+s,x_0) =\sigma^\mu (s,x_0) 
we realize that \sigma^\mu (t+s,x_0) and \sigma^\mu (t,\sigma (s,x_0)) satisfy the same equations with the same boundary conditions; therefore, we conclude that the flow can be composed. Take the modified flow at fixed time which send \mathcal{M} into itself:
\sigma_t:\mathcal{M}\mapsto\mathcal{M}
\sigma_t (x)\coloneqq \sigma (t,x)
then, we get the composition rules:
\sigma_s\circ\sigma_t = \sigma_{t+s}
and we have that:
\forall V\in\mathfrak{X}[\mathcal{M}],\exists \sigma_t \textrm{ flow}

Tangent (vector) fields V map a manifold into the corresponding tangent manifold:
V: \mathcal{M}\mapsto\mathcal{T}\mathcal{M}
Given that tangent manifolds are unions of tangent spaces:
\mathcal{T}\mathcal{M} \coloneqq \bigcup_{p\in\mathcal{M}} \mathcal{T}_p\mathcal{M}
and considering elements of the tangent spaces V_p\in \mathcal{T}_p\mathcal{M}, we can think of tangent fields as an expansion of the concept of tangent spaces, with V_p\mapsto V = V_p^\mu e_\mu and the V_p^\mu are functions of p.
We can define the set of all tangent fields:
\mathfrak{X}[\mathcal{M}] \coloneqq\left\{V: \mathcal{M}\mapsto\mathcal{T}\mathcal{M}\right\}

Taking the union of all tangent spaces \mathcal{T}_p\mathcal{M} of a manifold \mathcal{M} we get the tangent manifold of \mathcal{M}:
\mathcal{T}\mathcal{M} \coloneqq \bigcup_{p\in\mathcal{M}} \mathcal{T}_p\mathcal{M}
Locally, given a p and a neighborhood u sufficiently small, \mathcal{T}\mathcal{M} \simeq u\times \mathbb{R}^m, meaning that to describe the manifold it is sufficient to describe the manifold and the vector components.

QFT-in-Curved-Space-Time/Osterwalder-Sch.content:
If we have a Minkowski theory which is an analytical continuation of a euclidian one:
\textrm{Euclidian th. }\overset{\textrm{analytical cont.}}{\longleftrightarrow}\textrm{ Minkowski th.}
Then, the Minkowski theory is unitary if the euclidian one is reflection positive

Quantum-Information/Distances/Fidelity-a.content:
Useful relation between the notions of Fidelity and Trace Distance:
1-\sqrt{F(\rho_1,\rho _2)} \leq D(\rho_1,\rho _2) \leq\sqrt{1-F(\rho_1,\rho _2) }
For a proof see Holevo’s book, page 195.

Quantum-Information/Distances/Qubit-dist.content:
In the case of Qubits, using the Bloch sphere representation, we can define a notion of distance:
\rho _i =\frac{1}{2}\left(\id + \vec{r}_i\cdot\vec{\sigma}\right)
D(\rho _1,\rho _2) = \frac{1}{2} \abs{\vec{r}_1-\vec{r}_2}

Quantum-Information/Entanglement/Choi-st.content:
Given the Completely chaotic state in the system A:
\bar{\rho} _A = \frac{1}{d} \sum _i \ket{i}_A\bra{i}
where d =\dim\mathcal{H}_A Consider its Purification on the reference system R
\ket{\Psi}_{AR} =\frac{1}{\sqrt{d}}\sum _i \ket{i}_A\ket{i}_R
\ket{\Psi}_{AR} is a Bell state of AR. Then, the Choi state of a quantum channel \Phi is defined by:
\rho _\Phi \equiv \left[\Phi\otimes\chid_R\right](\ket{\Psi}_{AR}\bra{\Psi})= \frac{1}{d}\sum _{ij}\Phi \left(\ket{i}_A\bra{j}\right)\otimes \ket{i}_R\bra{j}

Quantum-Information/Entanglement/Complet.content:
The completely chaotic state is the state that maximizes the entropy of the system and are usually denoted by a bar \bar{\cdot} over the symbol of the operator
\bar{\rho} \equiv \frac{1}{\dim\mathcal{H}}\sum _i \op{i}{i}
Their purifications are the maximally entangled states.

Quantum-Information/Entanglement/Concurr.content:
Defined for Qubits.
X_{AB} (\rho _{AB}) \equiv\sqrt{\sqrt{\rho_{AB}}\left(\sigma_y^{(A)}\sigma_y^{(B)}\right)\rho_{AB} ^*\left(\sigma_y^{(A)}\sigma_y^{(B)}\right)\sqrt{\rho_{AB}}}
Here, \rho_{AB} ^* is defined by taking the element wise c.c. (requires fixing the basis - Computational basis).
Eigenvalues of X are ordered \lambda_1 \geq\lambda_2\geq \lambda_3\geq\lambda _4, then the concurrence is defined as
C(\rho_{AB}) \equiv \max\left\{0,\lambda_1-\lambda_2-\lambda _3-\lambda _4\right\}
