In the setting described in Classical Cryptography, Alice and Bob agree on the following protocol:

Bob produces a couple of strings: a public one x_{pub} and a private one (only known by him) x_{pr}.
The effectiveness of public-key schemes relies on the existence of an encoding such that, for a message \vec{m}:
E(\vec{m},\vec{x_{pub}}) = \vec{M}
so that the map M\mapsto m is a hard problem if only x_{pub} is known, and a decoding function such that:
D(\vec{M},\vec{x_{pr}}) = \vec{m}

Alice sends \vec{M} = E(\vec{m},\vec{x_{pub}}) to Bob
Eve cannot decrypt \vec{M}
Bob receives \vec{m} = D(\vec{M},\vec{x_{pr}})
This protocol is not unconditionally secure. RSA is an example of public-key cryptographic schemes.

