Let R be a ring and \{C_n\}_{n\in\mathbb{N}} be a family of R-modules, called chain modules or chain components. For C_n, the label n is called degree. Conventionally, we define C_{-1} = 0 the 0 module. Let
\left\{\partial _n: C_n \mapsto C_{n-1}\right\}_{n\in\mathbb{N}}
be a family of R-module homomorphisms, where \partial _n is called the n-th differential map or boundary map. We require that:
\partial _{n}\circ \partial _{n+1} = 0 \quad \forall n\in \mathbb{N}
or equivalently:
\mathrm{im} (\partial _{n+1}) \subseteq \mathrm{ker}(\partial _{n})\quad \forall n\in \mathbb{N}
Given the fact that the kernel and images of R-linear maps are R-modules, we can also conclude that \mathrm{im}(\partial _{n+1}) is a submodule of \mathrm{ker}(\partial _{n}).
This structure is called a chain complex of R-modules, denoted with C_\bullet.
If there exists m\in\mathbb{N} such that \forall n&gt; m, we have C_n = 0, then the chain complex is said to be bounded; we define the length of the chain complex as:
\mathrm{len}(C_\bullet) \coloneqq \min_{m\in \mathbb{N}}\left\{m: C_n = 0 \,\,\forall n&gt;m\right\}



                  Notation



When necessary, the differentials will be denoted together with their associated chain complex as \partial _n ^{C_\bullet}
